 Q. What’s a ConfigMap vs. Secret? How do you use them in Kubernetes deployments?
ANS : 

| Feature        | ConfigMap            | Secret                          |
| -------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Purpose        | Non-sensitive config | Sensitive data                  |
| Storage        | Plain text (etcd)    | Base64-encoded (can encrypt)    |
| Access Control | Less restrictive     | More restrictive                |
| Use Cases      | App configs, flags   | Passwords, tokens, certificates |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
🟢 What is a ConfigMap?
A ConfigMap is used to store non-sensitive configuration data in key-value pairs.

Example uses:
App configuration
Environment variables
Command-line arguments
Properties files
✅ Data is stored unencrypted.

🟢 What is a Secret?
A Secret is similar to a ConfigMap, but it’s meant to store sensitive data. 
like:
Passwords
API keys
TLS certificates
Kubernetes encodes Secrets in base64, and you can integrate encryption at rest.
✅ Access is restricted via RBAC.
-------------------------
🟢 How to Create Them
✅ ConfigMap Example
         ->  kubectl create configmap app-config-- from-literal=APP_MODE=production
✅ Secret Example

        -> kubectl create secret generic db-secret-- from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=MySecret123
----------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-app
        image: nginx
        env:
        - name: APP_MODE
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: app-config
              key: APP_MODE
        - name: DB_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: db-secret
              key: DB_PASSWORD
        volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /etc/config
        - name: secret-volume
          mountPath: /etc/secret
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: config-volume
        configMap:
          name: app-config
      - name: secret-volume
        secret:
          secretName: db-secret
