✅ Kubernetes PV & PVC Summary
🔹 What is a PV (PersistentVolume)?
A pre-created storage volume in the cluster.

Created by admins or automatically using a StorageClass.

Not attached to any pod by default.

🔹 What is a PVC (PersistentVolumeClaim)?
A request for storage made by a pod.

When a PVC matches a PV (by size, access mode, etc.), Kubernetes binds them.

🔸 Key Behavior:
A PV can only be bound to one PVC.

Even if the PVC requests less than the PV's size, the entire PV is reserved.

Unused space in a PV cannot be used by other pods.

To manage space efficiently, use dynamic provisioning with a StorageClass, which creates PVs of the exact size requested.

🧠 Example:
Resource	Size	Bound To	Notes
PV	10 Gi	PVC A	PVC A only asked for 5 Gi, but takes the full 10 Gi
PVC B	5 Gi	❌	Will not bind to PV (already taken)

✅ Best Practice:
Use dynamic provisioning so Kubernetes automatically creates just the right-sized volume per PVC request.
=--------------------
🧠 Important Concept:
A PersistentVolume (PV) can only be bound to ONE PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC).

Even if the PV is 10 GB and the PVC only asks for 5 GB, the entire PV is considered "used" once it's bound.

Kubernetes doesn’t share PVs across PVCs.

You cannot use the "leftover" 5 GB in that PV for another PVC.
-------------------------
What Happens?
Kubernetes binds the full PV to the PVC.

Even though the pod only asked for 5 GB, no other PVC can use the remaining 5 GB.

The full 10 GB is now reserved for that one PVC.

✔️ The pod can use up to 10 GB (not just 5), because it's bound to the full PV.
-----------------------------------------
What is Dynamic Provisioning?
With dynamic provisioning, Kubernetes automatically creates a PersistentVolume (PV) when you create a PVC — no need to create the PV yourself.

It uses a StorageClass, which defines the type and behavior of the storage (e.g., AWS EBS, GCP PD, local SSD, etc.).

✅ Steps to Use Dynamic Provisioning
🧾 1. Check Available StorageClasses
Run this command in your cluster:

bash
Copy
Edit
kubectl get storageclass
You might see output like:

bash
Copy
Edit
NAME            PROVISIONER             DEFAULT
gp2             kubernetes.io/aws-ebs   Yes
standard        pd.csi.storage.gke.io   No
The one with (default) will be used if you don’t specify a storageClassName.

🧾 2. Create a PVC That Uses a StorageClass
yaml
Copy
Edit
# file: pvc-dynamic.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-dynamic-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
  storageClassName: gp2  # Use the class from step 1
✅ This tells Kubernetes:

“Create a 5Gi PV automatically using the gp2 storage class (e.g., AWS EBS).”

🧾 3. Use the PVC in a Pod
yaml
Copy
Edit
# file: pod-using-pvc.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: app-using-pvc
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: nginx
      volumeMounts:
        - mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
          name: html-volume
  volumes:
    - name: html-volume
      persistentVolumeClaim:
        claimName: my-dynamic-pvc
🧾 4. Apply the YAML Files
bash
Copy
Edit
kubectl apply -f pvc-dynamic.yaml
kubectl apply -f pod-using-pvc.yaml
Kubernetes will:

Automatically create a PV using the StorageClass.

Bind the PVC to that new PV.

Your pod will use that storage.

✅ Benefits of Dynamic Provisioning
Feature	Benefit
No manual PV creation	Less work, fewer errors
Automatic right-size volumes	Avoid wasted storage
Supports many backends	EBS, GCE PD, NFS, Ceph, etc.
Clean-up on deletion	Optional (based on reclaim policy)

